WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:08.500
On September 6th, 2019, the 95-year-old former
president of Zimbabwe Robert Mugabe died of

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.580
cancer.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.940
He did not, however, die in Zimbabwe.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:19.819
Instead, Mugabe chose to receive his medical
care, as he always did, in Singapore — a

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.499
whole 5,000 miles or 8,000 kilometers away.

00:00:24.499 --> 00:00:30.539
Each of his many such trips required no fewer
than 10 hours in the air each way, by which

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.560
time he could’ve flown anywhere in continental
Europe.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.380
The African revolutionary was not the only
powerful person to seek treatment in this

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.140
southeast Asian city-state of just five million.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.220
So too did the daughter of the Philippines
president, the president of Bangladesh, several

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:53.800
military leaders of Myanmar, and the former
first lady of Indonesia, to name just a few

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.329
examples.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:00.950
Heads of state no doubt appreciate Singapore’s
tight control of the press and lack of protestors,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:07.759
though their first priority is surely the
same as its other 500,000 annual medical tourists

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:12.450
— to receive what is perhaps the best healthcare
on earth.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:18.960
Today the average Singaporean lives to be
an astonishing 83 years old — a full decade

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.119
longer than the world average, and only slightly
behind Switzerland, Macau, Japan, Hong Kong,

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:26.750
and San Marino.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:32.310
Another common metric for healthcare quality
is the infant mortality rate, at which Singapore

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:38.259
is among the best in the world — just 2.1
for every 1,000 live births.

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:44.090
Finally, Bloomberg ranked the country second
in overall efficiency.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.060
These are impressive outcomes on their own,
but they become downright amazing when you

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.859
consider how little they cost.

00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:57.810
The United States spends about 17% of its
GDP on healthcare — making it an embarrassing

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.310
outlier even among the most developed nations.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:10.220
The UK, a more representative example, stands
at 10%, Australia, 9, and Israel, just 8.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.410
Singapore, on the other hand, spends just
four percent of GDP.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:21.410
This is, in fact, lower than the minimum recommended
by the World Health Organization. On this

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:27.410
metric, Singapore is surrounded by poor countries
like Sudan, Eritrea, and Madagascar.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:32.090
If you didn’t know of its world-class outcomes,
you’d assume, based on this number, that

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:38.050
it must surely be a struggling backwater,
not a thriving global finance hub.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:43.660
If the US spent the same proportion of its
GDP on healthcare, it could afford, with those

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:50.310
savings, to permanently finance 139 of the
world’s smallest countries. It could save

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.060
three times the entire cost of the American
military.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:03:00.050
Somehow Singapore provides the same or even
better healthcare than the richest countries

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:05.050
in the world at the cost of the poorest countries
in the world.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:11.090
In designing healthcare systems, governments
willfully chose at most two of the following:

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.680
Price, quality, or equal access. They do so
because they accept these “natural” trade-offs

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.660
— believing that you can’t have it all.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.830
Singapore, on the other hand, seems to have
solved this M. C. Escher-level puzzle and

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.510
delivered all three.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:34.010
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the extended version of this

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.710
video.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:42.500
When Singapore gained independence in 1965,
healthcare was far from its highest priority.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.800
In the words of its health minister at the
time, it ranked at most fifth — after national

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.270
security, jobs, housing, and education.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:56.740
Facing an uncertain future and with the country’s
very existence at stake, the government had

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.620
no choice but to ration its attention and
resources.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:07.170
The U.S. performed its first successful kidney
transplant in the early 1950s. But Singapore

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.670
would have to wait twenty years to do the
same.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:16.190
With just 50 specialists for a population
of two million, it chose to focus first on

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:21.760
more pressing issues, like the rampant spread
of tuberculosis and pneumonia.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.700
This slow and methodical approach helped the
country develop a set of guiding, foundational

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.139
principles which informed its decision-making.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:35.560
While Singapore was still a British colony
in 1948 when the English National Health Service

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.790
was created, it chose a very different path.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:44.110
In fact, if there’s one philosophy that
defines its national approach to healthcare,

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.870
it’s a firm and unapologetic rejection of
what was perceived as the over-generosity

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:51.770
of the British.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:56.700
Believing that the general public was abusing,
misusing, and under-appreciating government

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.980
clinics, its first Prime Minister, Lee Kuan
Yew was quick to impose a fee.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.950
It was decided that while every citizen would
be guaranteed some form of care, nothing would

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:13.000
be strictly free, motivating a sense of personal
responsibility.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.970
But allergic as the government was to anything
resembling “welfare”, it also had little

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:23.090
confidence individuals could be trusted to
manage their own healthcare savings.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.280
The compromise to this tension was called
“MediSave”.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:33.050
The British had left Singapore with a compulsory
savings account known as the Central Provident

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.050
Fund.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:40.090
It required employees to deposit 5% of their
income, which employers matched, to be withdrawn

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.180
at retirement.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:47.690
Over the years, Lee raised these contribution
rates and introduced several new components.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:53.169
Today, the average Singaporean is required
to stash away about a third of their income

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:55.050
into three accounts.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:59.860
The first — the “Ordinary Account” — can
be used to pay for things like school and

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:00.860
housing.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.860
A second “Special Account” is reserved
for retirement, and merges with the Ordinary

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.080
Account at age 55.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:13.800
Finally, the MediSave account can be used
for medical care and insurance.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:19.150
In essence, every citizen is required to stash
away some money for a rainy day.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:23.670
Most income at the beginning of one’s career
is directed to the Ordinary Account, which

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:27.980
is one of the reasons so many Singaporeans
can afford their own home.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:32.370
As you approach retirement, more and more
of your money is sent to MediSave.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:37.669
And when the government runs a budget surplus,
it often simply deposits the difference directly

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:41.620
into these accounts, ensuring it’s put to
good use.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:46.780
In practice, the system functions neither
like American social security, nor a fully

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:52.930
self-owned bank account. It’s somewhere
in-between, which makes it somewhat controversial.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:58.040
On one hand, the government likes to boast
that citizens own their own funds. In some

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.670
sense, this is true. It’s not attached to
your employer, and thus doesn’t end if you’re

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:08.400
fired or laid off. The money it contains is
completely tax-free and can be willed away,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.340
like any property, at death.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:16.970
It’s also guaranteed a minimum, competitive,
interest rate between 2.5 and 5%.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.750
On the other hand, critics highlight how restrictive
it is.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:26.220
Funds are subject to both a minimum and maximum
balance, periodic limits, and deliberately

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.510
designed not to fully cover any single medical
expense, which the government argues would

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:34.110
eliminate personal responsibility.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:39.100
In other words, it’s entirely possible someone
could go broke from exorbitant medical bills

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:43.960
despite having plenty in their MediSave account
— for no other reason than the government

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.550
prohibits them from using it all.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:51.711
Every day of hospitalization, procedure, and
treatment has a maximum proportion that can

00:07:51.711 --> 00:07:56.710
be paid for by MediSave — the rest is simply
out of pocket.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.680
In 1990, the Singapore government addressed
this inadequacy and introduced what they had

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.509
been trying to avoid — insurance.

00:08:04.509 --> 00:08:09.640
MediShield Life, as it’s now known, is intended
as a defense against only the most catastrophic

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:15.420
of conditions involving long-term treatment,
like dialysis or cancer therapy.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.370
Even then, it activates only after patients
have contributed a significant portion either

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.880
out of pocket or via their MediSave account.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.889
But it’s very affordable.

00:08:25.889 --> 00:08:32.110
A 20-year-old, for example, pays just $101
US Dollars a year, which can even be paid

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.200
for with MediSave.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:40.180
Each age group pays however much is needed
to make it independently sustainable. In other

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:45.089
words, it’s not simply a transfer of wealth
from the young to the old, like the American

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:48.480
pyramid scheme known as “Social Security”.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.870
Because of this, older people pay much higher
premiums. $509 a year, for example, for those

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.240
aged 90 or older.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:03.460
Still, MediShield is generally well-liked.
Though compulsory today, more people participated

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:10.050
when it was voluntary than in the U.S., three
years after ObamaCare was made mandatory — 94%

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.399
to 91.

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:16.730
Those who want more comprehensive insurance
have the option of buying upgraded plans from

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:21.300
private companies, all of which are tightly
regulated to ensure competition.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:28.990
Finally, in 1993, Singapore stepped in with
a safety net of last resort for the lowest-income

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.100
households.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:35.920
MediFund is a principal-protected, and thus
indefinitely sustainable, endowment which

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:39.580
pays for the medical care of those who can’t.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:44.060
Any citizen who can prove their inability
to pay can apply to a group of independent

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:47.990
volunteers who make case-by-case judgments.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:52.770
Most applications are approved within 30 minutes,
but the most complicated of cases can take

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.810
up to three weeks.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:10:02.350
In 2019, MediFund paid $120 million involving
over a million applications.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:08.090
These three “M’s” — MediSave, MediShield,
and MediFund, are often cherry-picked by those

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:13.850
seeking to reform healthcare abroad. Some
point to the mandatory savings accounts as

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:19.339
evidence healthcare shouldn’t be handed
out for “free”. Others protest that MediFund

00:10:19.339 --> 00:10:21.540
is the real backbone of the system.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:28.360
In truth, neither is completely accurate.
Each of the three M’s is one layer of protection

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.589
— without which the entire system wouldn’t
function.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:36.380
But don’t get the wrong impression. The
bulk of healthcare spending still takes the

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:38.400
form of subsidies.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:47.400
In 2013, MediSave, MediShield, and Medifund
accounted for 5.1, 2.1, and 0.8% of all healthcare

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.250
expenditures, respectively.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:55.959
What’s revolutionary about the 3Ms, however,
is not how large they are, but how they control

00:10:55.959 --> 00:11:00.020
costs for everyone — including taxpayers.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:05.400
Singapore solves the American problem in which
hospitals are incentivized to charge exorbitant

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.870
prices which are merely passed on to insurance
companies.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:16.230
Because patients pay more out of pocket, hospitals
are forced to control costs. These lower costs

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:20.630
are passed on to the government, too, when
it distributes subsidies.

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:26.360
In other words, while the sticker price may
feel higher for Singaporeans, the total amount

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:32.399
they pay, both out-of-pocket and in taxes,
is far, far lower.

00:11:32.399 --> 00:11:38.200
All of this is enabled by transparency. The
government makes all costs public and easily

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:43.720
accessible online. Hospitals are required
to provide patients with an estimate of their

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:48.180
bill, as well as the average cost at competing
hospitals.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:53.910
But how, you may ask, are these subsidies
distributed? What system could possibly ensure

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:58.050
that the poor get support while the rich don’t
abuse it?

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.300
For the most part, people just sort themselves.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:07.810
Public hospital rooms are split into four
classes: C, B2, B1, and A.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:15.350
The cheapest, Class C, have 8-10 beds in a
fairly utilitarian room, while the most expensive,

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:19.750
Class A, are private rooms with a TV and Air
Conditioning.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:26.460
The important thing is these classes are treated
as cosmetic, luxury upgrades. The same doctors

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:32.290
operate all wards with the same standards
of care. They have no monetary incentive to

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:34.100
treat anyone differently.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:40.370
And much like airplane classes, more premium
rooms cost significantly more with only modest

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:45.690
improvements, which naturally separates patients
based on their willingness to pay.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:51.320
Is it fair that the poor sit in a stuffy,
crowded room during hot, humid summers? Answers

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:57.460
will vary, but many would argue the alternative:
high prices for everyone, is worse.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:02.450
In fact, so many patients were content choosing
lower classes than they could afford, that

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:08.279
hospitals eventually introduced means-testing.
Anyone, with any income, can choose whatever

00:13:08.279 --> 00:13:14.350
room they prefer, but subsidies vary both
based on ward class and income.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:21.830
Class A rooms, for example, offer no subsidy,
while class C are subsidized up to 80%.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:26.140
For all these reasons, Singapore defies categorization.

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:31.470
Is it a free-market, or is it centrally planned?
Yes.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:36.410
In theory, hospitals are welcome to charge
whatever, and yet the entire market is tightly

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:42.100
controlled by the government, who, in regulating
everything from MediSave minimum contributions,

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:47.300
to what qualifies for MediShield, can influence
whatever outcome it wishes.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.209
Is it public or private? Yes.

00:13:50.209 --> 00:13:57.080
20% of primary care and 80% of secondary is
public, but the two are expressly designed

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:58.650
to work in concert.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:01.730
The system is not perfect.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:07.140
One challenge is that MediSave heavily relies
on families who pool together costs for major

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:08.300
operations.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:15.390
In 2010, for example, 44% of withdrawals were
made for a family member. For example, someone’s

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:17.690
child, spouse, or parent.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:23.839
Today, however, the average Singaporean woman
has just 1.15 children in her lifetime — the

00:14:23.839 --> 00:14:29.690
third lowest fertility rate in the world — meaning
the population is rapidly shrinking.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:35.530
As the country gets older, it will experience
significantly more chronic and complex illnesses,

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:40.329
yet smaller families will mean fewer people
can pool savings.

00:14:40.329 --> 00:14:45.330
Solving healthcare is an ongoing process.
But Singapore can credibly say that it has

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:51.760
paved a new, third path. Some countries offer
medicine free-at-the-point-of-care, which

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:57.280
results in long queues, others simply don’t
guarantee healthcare at all, and therefore

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:59.940
have to leave behind the most vulnerable.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:03.880
Singapore, on the other hand, does neither.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:10.810
The million-dollar question is whether Singapore
is a strange anomaly or a viable model for

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:15.760
the rest of the world? Could this system work
in the United States, for example?

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:21.610
That’s the question we explore in the extended
